## 2026-08-07 — clean-engineering-model.md (fidelity sections instead of modules)

**Artifact:** `sandbox/courier/.context/clean-engineering-model.md` and `context_tools/clean_engineering/templates/clean_engineering-templates.md`
**Rule:** `clean_engineering-templates` and `markdown_class_model.py` document format: H1 = module, H2 = class within that module; organize by module, not by fidelity level

**Wrong:** Document had ONE H1 (the full system title) with `## Model fidelity` and `## Specification fidelity` as H2 headings, grouping ALL interfaces together and ALL implementations together. The markdown parser (`markdown_class_model.py`) reads H1 as module and H2 as class — so "Model fidelity" and "Specification fidelity" became two giant class boxes in Draw.io, each containing all interface or all implementation content crammed into one cell. Additionally, inline `<!-- Md -->` and `<!-- S -->` tags on property lines get included in the type_hint strings, polluting the parsed model with comment text. The template (`clean_engineering-templates.md`) has the same wrong structure.

**Correct:** The document must use H1 per module (`# routes`, `# zones`, `# packages`) and H2 per class (`## IRoute`, `## Route`, `## IRouteZoneSequence`, etc.). Interface (I{Type}) and implementation ({Type}) both sit under the same module H1 — interface first, then implementation. No `## Model fidelity` or `## Specification fidelity` section headers anywhere. Fidelity is conveyed by the class name convention (I{Type} vs {Type}) and the `+` prefix on spec implementations, not by grouping headings. Language companion and modules overview go as prose BEFORE the first H1 (parser ignores pre-H1 content but humans can read it). The template must reflect this same module-first structure.

**When:** spec / generate / gen-ce pass — followed the template which incorrectly grouped by fidelity instead of by module; template itself encoded the wrong structure

